    "created_at": "2021-05-01T07:47:35Z",
    "updated_at": "2021-05-01T07:47:35Z",
    "body": "Analytics is always a controversial topic. It's harder for open source projects to get feedback from users than commercials because they _usually_ don't track you, and even if they do, _most_ of them only collect necessary info (e.g. crash reports) instead of tracking _actively_.\r\n[Many](https://github.com/search?q=google+analytics&type=commits) [open source](https://docs.brew.sh/Analytics) [projects](https://yarnpkg.com/advanced/telemetry) choose to use a closed source analytics (e.g. Google Analytics, Datadog) because they are **free** and **reliable**, so maintainers don't need to spend extra money and time on it.\r\n\r\nI can't speak for Starship, but here are some of possible choices:\r\n\r\n- Self-hosted analytics. The optimal choice, but won't work for most projects.\r\n- Improve the current way of collecting data. For example, using a open source _implementation_ of Google Analytics, such as [ga-lite](https://github.com/jehna/ga-lite).\r\n- Opt-in analytics, like [pkgstats](https://wiki.archlinux.org/index.php/pkgstats).\r\n- Drop the analytics completely because it's not worth it, like [neovim](https://github.com/neovim/neovim.github.io/issues/183#issuecomment-651260192).",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
